  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nA more active weather pattern will develop Wednesday and continue\ninto Friday. Mid level ridging currently in place across the region \nwill begin to break down tonight into Wednesday as some mid level \nenergy moves out of the mid Mississippi Valley and into the Ohio \nValley. This will allow for a weak frontal boundary to sag slowly \nsoutheast into our area through the day on Wednesday. The boundary \nwill likely stall out somewhere along the Ohio River Wednesday night \nbefore beginning to lift back to the north as a warm front through \nthe day on Thursday as a weak surface wave rides east along it.\nFinally, a stronger mid level short wave will help carve out a sharp\nupper level trough across the Great Lakes region Thursday night into\nFriday, with an associated strong cold front moving southeast across\nour area Friday morning into early afternoon. \n\nDeveloping moisture advection ahead of the boundary on Wednesday will\nlead to increasing PWs, climbing into the 2-2.25 inch range through \nWednesday afternoon and then remaining high along and south of the \nboundary into Thursday. Given the increasing moisture and forcing, \nexpect widespread showers and embedded thunderstorms to develop \nWednesday afternoon and continue into Wednesday evening with some of \nthe precipitation likely lingering through the night south of the \nboundary.\n\nTo the south of the boundary, temperatures will remain unseasonably \nwarm during the day on Wednesday with highs ranging from the upper \n70s north to the mid 80s across our far south. This will allow for \nsome MLCapes of around 1000 J/KG or so, mainly to the south of the \nI-70 corridor through Wednesday afternoon. With the strengthening mid\nlevel flow, deep layer shear will increase through the day on \nWednesday, so a few strong to severe thunderstorms will be possible \nWednesday afternoon into the evening, with damaging wind the main \nthreat. The best chance for this will be across our southern areas, \nbut the overall severe threat will be contingent on how well we are \nable to destabilize through the day. \n\nLocally heavy rainfall will also be possible given the high PWs. \nInitially, storms look to be somewhat progressive Wednesday \nafternoon, but as the boundary begins to stall out, some training \nstorms may begin to develop, leading to some potential flooding \nconcerns heading into Wednesday night.\n\nChances for showers and storms will again be possible Thursday\nafternoon into Thursday evening as the frontal boundary lifts back \nto the north. Good deep layer shear will again be present so if we \nare able to destabilize enough, some additional strong to severe \nstorms would be possible Thursday. \n\nAs the strong cold front moves southeast across the area, good low \nlevel CAA will develop Friday morning into the afternoon. In the\nsoutherly flow ahead of this, temperatures will remain mild Thursday\nnight before dropping during the day on Friday as the colder air \nmoves in. In addition, the strong CAA will support gusty winds to \naround 40 mph or so during the day on Friday. \n\nKey Message 2)\n\nA much colder airmass will continue to settle into the area for the \nweekend. Lows Friday night and Saturday night will be in the 30s with\nthe potential for some frost, especially Saturday night when winds\nwill be lighter.",
